  "content": {
    "id": "p22-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Find the value of $h$ for which the graph of $y=-3^x+h$ has exactly one point of intersection with the graph of $y=3^{-x}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$h=2$.\n\nPut $t=3^x>0$; the intersection quadratic must have one positive root.",
      "worked": "At an intersection,\n$$\\begin{align*}-3^x+h&=3^{-x}\\\\3^{2x}-h3^x+1&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nPut $t=3^x>0$. Then $t^2-ht+1=0$. Each positive root gives exactly one value of $x$.\n\nThe roots have product $1$, so two distinct real roots have the same sign. Exactly one positive root therefore requires a repeated positive root.\n$$\\begin{align*}h^2-4&=0\\\\h&=\\pm2.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $h=2$, $(t-1)^2=0$, giving $t=1$, $x=0$ and $y=1$.\n\nFor $h=-2$, $(t+1)^2=0$, giving $t=-1$, which is impossible because $t>0$.\n\nTherefore $h=2$, with the single intersection $(0,1)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
